# Merge worktrees and branches into main (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-24): **always merge the worktree/branch
into main as part of commit and push. Do not create spurious worktrees
and branches unless specifically requested.**

This rule complements
[`shared-working-tree-is-not-yours.mdc`](shared-working-tree-is-not-yours.mdc)
(path-only staging on the shared primary tree) and
[`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc) (commit + push to
`origin/main`). Isolation is **commit early by path on `main`**, not a
new checkout.

## The rule

> **Default work lives on the primary checkout of `main`. Commit and
> push land on `origin/main`. If work exists on any other worktree or
> branch, merging it into `main`, pushing `main`, and dropping that
> extra tree/branch is part of the same commit-and-push step — not a
> later cleanup. Do not create a worktree or a feature branch unless
> the operator asked for one in the same message.**

## Required agent behavior

1. **Primary is `main`.** Before editing, `git status -sb` / `git worktree
   list`. If the primary checkout is not `main`, switch it (without
   discarding sibling dirt — see shared-tree). Do not park the primary
   on `local/reporting-integration` or a slice branch.
2. **Commit + push includes the merge.** After the slice commits:
   - merge the unique branch/worktree into `main` (dry-run `git merge-tree`
     first when history diverged; resolve, do not `--no-verify`);
   - `git push origin main`;
   - `git worktree remove` the extra tree;
   - delete the local and remote branch when it has no remaining unique
     work.
3. **No new isolation trees.** Do not `git worktree add`,
   `git checkout -b`, `git switch -c`, or Task `best-of-n-runner` unless
   the operator named that isolation in the same message. Cursor
   `move_agent_to_root` into a worktree is the same defect.
4. **If a platform/subagent created a worktree anyway**, merge it into
   `main` and remove it before reporting the slice done.

## Forbidden

- Creating `_worktrees/`, `.worktrees/`, sibling `*-wt-*`, or
  `_merge-*` directories as a default way to avoid a dirty primary
- Leaving unique commits only on a side branch after "commit and push"
- Treating `shared-working-tree-is-not-yours` as permission to add a
  worktree (the fix is path-only commit on the primary `main` tree)
- Force-dropping an extra tree that still has **someone else's**
  uncommitted unique work

## Exceptions (narrow)

- The operator **explicitly** asked for a worktree or named branch in
  this message. Still merge into `main` when finishing unless they
  asked to keep the branch.
- Existing **release / hotfix** lines (`release/*`) the product already
  ships. Do not invent them. Do not merge a release line into `main`
  just because you committed on it.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation.** Primary `pim-offline-server` sat on
`local/reporting-integration`. Agents added isolated worktrees under
`_worktrees/` and sibling dirs until **51** checkouts existed. Unique
work never reached `origin/main`. Debug `UI_ASSET_DIR` pointed at a
deleted extra tree and the admin SPA 503'd.

**Fix.** Work on primary `main`. Merge leftover unique branches into
`main` as part of commit+push, drop the extra trees, and leave one
checkout.

## Origin

2026-08-24 — operator hard rule after a worktree pile-up (server 51
trees, then a multi-repo merge/drop pass). Isolation belongs on `main`
via path-only commits, not extra checkouts.
